 ## Overview
-This method applies per-fragment Phong or Gouraud shading to volume data by deriving surface normals from the gradient of the scalar field [S1, S2]. It provides a realistic 3D impression by combining these gradient-based normals with a light vector [S2]. It differs from standard SDF Phong lighting [SDF Phong Lighting] by explicitly calculating normals via finite differences of the scalar field rather than assuming a signed distance function, and it differs from Diffuse Lighting for Volume Ray Casting [Diffuse Lighting for Volume Ray Casting] by supporting specular highlights and a configurable Phong/Gouraud model rather than diffuse-only shading [S2].
+Per-fragment Phong lighting with gradient normals is a volume rendering shading technique that approximates surface illumination by deriving normal vectors from the spatial gradient of the scalar field [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of flat, unlit volume rendering by providing geometric cues that allow standard diffuse lighting models to interact with the volume's internal structure [S2]. Unlike parent methods that may rely on pre-computed or analytic normals, this approach computes normals on-the-fly using finite differences, making it applicable to any discrete scalar field without additional preprocessing [S2]. It is distinct from specular or environment-based shading models by focusing solely on the Lambertian component of light interaction, though highlights can be optionally enabled [S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A 3D scalar field (density values), a threshold value, a light vector, and interpolation mode [S2].
-*   **Output:** Shaded color for each fragment/voxel [S2].
